‘Sanctions hit an aggressor's economy because, without export revenue, it lacks the money for rearmament – Rotland's state budget loses billions when the buyers of its raw materials disappear.’ {{1}}
‘Sanctions never achieve anything anyway; everyone knows that.’ {{2}}
‘Sanctions often hurt the population rather than the leadership because rising prices and shortages affect the most vulnerable first – in Rotland, medicines become scarce while the leadership arranges its imports through indirect routes.’ {{3}}
‘Anyone who criticises sanctions automatically sides with the aggressor.’ {{4}}
‘Sanctions send a signal without weapons because they visibly put a price on breaking the rules – even states without armies can freeze accounts and take a stand.’ {{5}}
‘Sanctions work too slowly to stop a war because states build up stocks and find new trading partners – Rotland's stockpiles keep its economy afloat for years.’ {{6}}
‘Forcefulness is the only language that is understood.’ {{7}}
‘Sanctions strengthen international unity because joint pressure is more effective than individual measures – if dozens of states join in, Rotland's leadership struggles to find banks to handle its business.’ {{8}}
‘Sanctions can be circumvented through third countries because goods can flow through countries that do not take part – the fictional country Gelbland becomes a hub for Rotland's purchases.’ {{9}}
‘In the end, these things always sort themselves out.’ {{10}}
